WEBVTT

00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:07.297
An anti-join pattern answers a practical question: which rows on the left have no related row on the right?

00:00:07.297 --> 00:00:11.897
In this example, we want patients who do not have any lab results.

00:00:11.897 --> 00:00:15.702
We start with a LEFT JOIN so every patient is preserved.

00:00:15.702 --> 00:00:21.002
If a patient has no matching lab result, the lab-side columns become NULL.

00:00:21.002 --> 00:00:26.207
Then we filter for a right-side key, such as test_id, being NULL.

00:00:26.207 --> 00:00:29.427
The choice of column in the NULL test matters.

00:00:29.427 --> 00:00:35.027
A primary key is a good choice because a real matched row should always have that value.

00:00:35.027 --> 00:00:42.257
If you tested a column that could legitimately be NULL, you might incorrectly label a matched record as

00:00:42.257 --> 00:00:45.797
unmatched. This pattern is extremely useful in real projects.

00:00:45.797 --> 00:00:49.592
An e-commerce company can find customers with no orders.

00:00:49.592 --> 00:00:53.577
A university can find students with no course registration.

00:00:53.577 --> 00:00:57.342
A hospital can find patients with no follow-up visit.

00:00:57.342 --> 00:01:00.817
A warehouse can find products with no sales.

00:01:00.817 --> 00:01:05.362
Read the logic in plain English: keep all left-side records, attempt the match,

00:01:05.362 --> 00:01:10.092
and then keep only the records where no right-side row was found.
